      "message": "\u003e I believe the frame sharing problem is possible but shall not affecting esp-encrypt: the next node was set by esp_encrypt_inline as metadata and later retrieved in post node. So no way it will be invalid.\n\nNo sure I follow this explanation? The failure is not that encrypt packet’s own per-buffer next_index gets non-corrupted, but rather a decrypt packet be delivered to an encrypt post node, where decrypt metadata is interpreted as an encrypt next index.\n\nI can also reproduce a segfault with esp-encrypt that mirrors what this change is fixing. Here is a repro sequence:\n\n1. esp4-decrypt-tun places decrypt buffers in a pending crypto-enq frame.\n2. Before that frame runs, esp4-encrypt-tun obtains the same incomplete frame.\n3. The helper appends the encrypt buffers and overwrites the frame scalar with ENCRYPT plus esp4-encrypt-tun-post.\n4. All buffers—including the decrypt buffers—are processed and returned to esp4-encrypt-tun-post.\n5. Encrypt and decrypt post metadata share a union: encryption stores a u16 next_index, while decryption stores a structure beginning with a buffer pointer. See esp.h.\n6. The encrypt post node therefore interprets the low 16 bits of the decrypt buffer pointer as its next-node index. It passes that invalid value to vlib_buffer_enqueue_to_next(). See esp_encrypt.c.\n\nI then get a segfault with a backtrace of:\nvlib_get_next_frame_internal\nvlib_buffer_enqueue_to_next_fn_x86_64_v4\nesp4_encrypt_tun_post_node_fn_x86_64_v4\n\nDisassembling the exact bad instruction in libvlib.so.26.06 places segfault here which agrees with the explanation that the frame metadata is being interpreted improperly:\nnext_node \u003d nm-\u003enodes[node-\u003enext_nodes[next_index]];",
